  "result": "JPY and KRW invoices are now charged at their face value: ¥1,500 goes to the gateway as 1500, not 150000. `npm test` passes with 8 of 8 tests, including the new ones.\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cased the currency (`jpy`) for the gateway and then passed that lower-case code to `toMinor`. The currency registry is keyed by upper-case codes, so the lookup missed and fell back to the default of 2 decimal places. Zero-decimal currencies were multiplied by 100. USD and EUR use 2 decimals, so they came out right by accident.\n\n**Fix:** `toMinor` now receives `invoice.currency`, the stored upper-case code. The lower-case code is still what goes in the payload's `currency` field.\n\n**Regression tests** in `test/charge.test.js`:\n- JPY 1500 gives 1500, with currency `jpy`.\n- KRW 20000 gives 20000.\n- KWD 1.5 gives 1500, covering a 3-decimal currency.\n\n**Not changed:** `exponent()` in `src/lib/currency/registry.js` still silently defaults to 2 decimals for any code it doesn't recognise, including a wrong-case one. That default is what turned this bug into a wrong charge instead of an error. I left it alone because the other callers (refunds, payouts, dunning, coupons, proration) pass the stored upper-case code and are fine. Making it throw on unknown codes would be a sensible follow-up, if you want it.",
